# [Generate an extension]($heading.id('generate-extension'))

Run these commands in an empty working directory on Linux or macOS. Download the skeleton generator before running it; this also works with PHP 8.2:

```sh
curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/happystraw/phpz/dev/tools/phpz_skel.php
php phpz_skel.php --ext my_php_extension --with-php-config "$(command -v php-config)"
cd my_php_extension
```

The generator calls `zig init`, adds the phpz dependency, and generates the templates. The target directory contains `my_php_extension.stub.php`, `my_php_extension.h`, and `src/root.zig`.

# [Build and call]($heading.id('build-and-call'))

```sh
zig build -Dphp-include-dir="$(php-config --include-dir)"
php -n -dextension=./modules/my_php_extension.so -r 'hello();'
php -n -dextension=./modules/my_php_extension.so -r 'echo greet("World"), PHP_EOL;'
```

The two PHP commands print:

```
Hello from Zig!
Hello, World!
```

The `-n` option skips php.ini for this CLI invocation. The `-dextension` option loads the extension for the current process.

# [Connect the function]($heading.id('connect-the-function'))

The Stub declares the PHP function signature:

```php
function hello(): void {}
```

Its Zig implementation lives in the `functions` container:

```zig
pub fn hello() void {
    _ = phpz.printf("Hello from Zig!\n", .{});
}
```

At compile time, `phpz.functions(functions, .{})` exports the handlers and `phpz.module(...)` exports the module. The function table generated from the Stub connects the PHP functions to these handlers.

# [Run tests]($heading.id('run-tests'))

```sh
zig build run-tests -Dphp-include-dir="$(php-config --include-dir)"
```

The skeleton includes tests for `hello`, `greet`, and `Counter`. After changing a PHP signature, run `zig build gen-stub` before rebuilding and testing.

On Windows, the extension is `modules/php_my_php_extension.dll`. For build options, switch to 简体中文 and open Windows in the sidebar.

